# Is our per-user-only access actually a problem?

## Short answer

No, technically it's fine. The blocker is policy, layered on top of a structural reality.

## What the transcript actually says

**Georg (MS) at 21:16** made your point exactly:
> "it will say exactly what permissions are given, and… it should only be like access to read the user who is currently trying to sign in… it should just be a standard OAuth permission flow."

**Digvijay at 21:43:**
> "Yeah, that is understandable. But… we have around 250,000 users… we are only responsible for KPMG Sweden, not for KPMG Global. And this is why these kind of configuration is not permissible by the KPMG Global."

Digvijay isn't disputing the technical minimality. He's saying it doesn't matter — KPMG Global's rule is **categorical, not risk-graded**.

## Two separate things going on

### 1. Technical reality — you're right

Standard OIDC sign-in with delegated `openid profile email` only sees the user who actually signed in. Normain can't list, search, or enumerate the directory. The "250k users" number is misleading on a data-exposure basis — Normain only ever touches the few hundred KPMG Sweden users who actively log in. Millions of SaaS apps run on exactly this model.

### 2. Structural problem KPMG Sweden has

KPMG Sweden does not have its own tenant. They share the KPMG Global tenant with 120+ member firms. To create *any* service principal — even one with zero permissions — someone has to write into the Global tenant. That requires Global Admin approval. KPMG Global has a blanket "no third-party apps consented in the global tenant" rule.

So the gate isn't "is Normain's access minimal?" — it's "does a third-party SP get created here at all?"

Aleksandar and Digvijay can't override this. NITSO is the only path, and they don't approve via auto-consent regardless of permission scope.

## Is this just KPMG being difficult?

No — standard at this scale. Big-4, banks, defence, healthcare, large gov almost always refuse Microsoft's admin-consent flow for third-party SaaS, regardless of permission scope. Why:

- **Audit posture.** Global admin doesn't want their click-through history to be the approval mechanism for hundreds of vendor apps a year.
- **Scope-creep prevention.** Auto-consent SPs can request more permissions later via incremental consent. Manual creation locks scope at provisioning time.
- **Enforcement KPMG controls.** Manual SP with user-assignment-required = Microsoft itself blocks non-assigned users at sign-in, before tokens reach Normain. Auto-consent leaves restriction up to post-hoc config.
- **Local autonomy.** Sweden wants to roll out SaaS without lining up at Global's door each time.

## Takeaway

If Normain were selling to a 500-person startup, admin consent would be fine. At KPMG scale + federation, this conversation will recur with every Big-4 / large bank / large enterprise customer.

Worth treating "federate against a customer-owned SP" as a **first-class enterprise feature** in Normain's product, not a workaround for KPMG. Same path will unlock Deloitte, PwC, EY, big banks, etc.
